{
  "result": "There is an hour just before dawn when the world holds its breath. The sky is a deep, bruised blue, streetlights are still humming orange, and everything is quiet except for the occasional delivery truck rumbling by. I love that hour, not because I am particularly productive in it, but because it feels borrowed, as if time hasn't quite started yet.\n\nI used to think stillness meant emptiness. I was wrong. Stillness is full. If you sit long enough in a quiet room, you start to hear things you missed \u2014 the soft tick of the refrigerator, the faint creak of the house settling, the wind brushing against the window like it wants to be let in. Our lives are usually so loud we forget how much is happening in the silence.\n\nLately I've been collecting small, unimportant moments. The way sunlight makes dust visible in the kitchen at 4 p.m. The perfect weight of a chipped ceramic mug. The way a stranger holds the door an extra second for you even when your hands are full. None of these will make headlines or change history, but they are the texture of being alive.\n\nWe are taught to chase big things \u2014 achievements, milestones, grand adventures. And those matter. But I think a good life is mostly made of tiny, ordinary mercies that we barely notice until they are gone. So I try to notice them now, while they are still here, for as long as I possibly can.",
